th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-part-1.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-part-1.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_51e80d58_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f(x) = x^2$ and $F(x) = \\int_0^x t^2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, what is $F'(x)$?","options":["$2x$","$\\dfrac{x^3}{3}$","$0$","$x^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"51e95836924848337960f807","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_51e80d58_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $f$ is Riemann integrable on $[a,b]$ but not known to be continuous at some interior point $x_0$. What can Part 1 still guarantee?","options":["$F'(x_0)=f(x_0)$, as at every other point of $(a,b)$.","$f$ is continuous on $[a,b]$, since it is integrable.","$F$ is continuous only away from the point $x_0$.","$F(x) = \\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ is continuous on $[a,b]$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7388ba31f264fe00db22de68","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_51e80d58_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is the derivative at $x=0$ of $G(x)=\\displaystyle\\int_{2x}^{\\cos x}\\mathrm{e}^{t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$?","options":["$-2\\mathrm{e}$","$-\\mathrm{e}$","$2\\mathrm{e}^2$","$-2\\mathrm{e}^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b5b924fb7989cd8445b7c20b","time_limit":30},{"id":"fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_expansion_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What function is built from $f$ in Part 1 of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus?","options":["$F(x)=f'(x)$","$F(x)=f(a)+f(b)$","$F(x)=\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$","$F(x)=\\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8a0e3925f8b518a128f701c1","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_51e80d58_002_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f$ be Riemann integrable on $[a,b]$, $F(x)=\\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $x_0\\in(a,b)$. Which assumption on its own guarantees $F'(x_0)=f(x_0)$?","options":["$f$ is continuous at $x_0$.","$f$ is bounded on $[a,b]$.","$F$ is linear on $[a,b]$.","$F$ is continuous at $x_0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b3293d0a77bb6fc1e127dea1","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_51e80d58_009_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Part 1 states $F'(x_0)=f(x_0)$ only for $x_0\\in(a,b)$, where $F(x)=\\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ on $[a,b]$. Why are $a$ and $b$ excluded?","options":["At $a$ and $b$, $x$ can approach from one side only.","$F$ is not continuous at $a$ and $b$.","Every integrable $f$ is discontinuous at $a$ and $b$.","$F(a)=0$, so $F$ has no derivative at $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a9070d196e3f9df44315cd46","time_limit":40},{"id":"fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_expansion_006_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be continuous. What is $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\displaystyle\\int_0^{x^2}f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$?","options":["$f(x^2)$","$2x f(x^2)$","$x^2 f(x)$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"06445703ad8b47fb68f3931a","time_limit":20},{"id":"fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_expansion_007_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"On $[0,2]$, let $f(1)=1$ and $f(x)=0$ for $x\\ne1$, and let $F(x)=\\int_0^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Which statement is correct?","options":["$F$ is identically $0$, so $F'(1)=0\\ne f(1)$.","$F'(1)=f(1)=1$, by Part 1 of the theorem.","$F$ is not continuous at $x=1$, where $f$ jumps.","$F$ has a corner at $x=1$, so $F'(1)$ does not exist."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1a4030730fb5a7f55c63250a","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_51e80d58_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"On $[0,2]$ let $f(t)=0$ for $0\\le t<1$ and $f(t)=1$ for $1\\le t\\le2$, and let $F(x)=\\int_0^x f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. What are the left-hand and right-hand derivatives of $F$ at $x=1$?","options":["Left $0$, right $1$; so $F'(1)$ does not exist.","Both equal $f(1)=1$, so $F'(1)=1$.","Both equal $\\tfrac{1}{2}$, so $F'(1)=\\tfrac{1}{2}$.","Neither exists, since $F$ jumps at $x=1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3e09bb7f00dbc540bda2248f","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_fundamental_theorem_of_calculus_part_1_51e80d58_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $F(x)=\\int_0^x (t-1)(t-3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ for $x\\in[0,5]$. Which point of $(0,5)$ is a local maximum point of $F$?","options":["$x=2$","$x=3$","$x=1$","both $x=1$ and $x=3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"31f0d1828dff192935d877cd","time_limit":30}]}}